          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C \to D
            is a generalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %p
            A basic fact in ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon C \to D
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            – in that there is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon D \to C
            and natural isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ g \simeq Id_D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f \simeq Id_C
            – if and only if it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is obtained by
            %em choosing
            for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
            an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(c) \simeq d
          %p
            The notion of
            %em anafunctor
            is a generalization of the usual notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em functor
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Makkai"} Makkai
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundational concerns
            in mind, although it also appears unnamed in (
            %a{:href => "#Kelly"}> Kelly
            ). Later, they were applied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal categories
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilsum-Skandalis+morphism"} Hilsum-Skandalis morphism
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
          %p We present three motivations and applications of anafunctors:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FoundationalMotivation"} Anafunctors as a tool for handling foundational issues
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#InternalCatMotivation"} Anafunctors as morphisms in internal categories
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#HomotopicalMotivation"} Anafunctors as morphisms in a homotopy category
          %h3#FoundationalMotivation Foundational motivation
          %p
            There is a sense in which the construction of inverse equivalences is not a “real” use of the axiom of choice, because the choice of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is determined up to unique isomorphism. Note that in ordinary set theory, the axiom of choice is not necessary to make choices that are uniquely defined; this is sometimes called the “axiom of non-choice” or the “function comprehension principle.” Since in category theory, an object can only be expected to be determined up to unique isomorphism, it is natural to regard the above statement as really being a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+comprehension+principle"}> functor comprehension principle
            ” or an “axiom of non-choice for categories.” The fact that the full axiom of choice is required to make such choices is then an artifact of the usual
            = succeed "al" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of objects.
          %p
            In fact, however, we can recover the functor comprehension principle while maintaining the definition of categories in set theory if we modify the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em functor
            = succeed "," do
              %em anafunctor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            (in the sense of having an inverse
            = succeed ")" do
              %em anafunctor
          %p
            Every functor may be interpreted as an anafunctor; that every anafunctor is equivalent to a functor is equivalent to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            (without using the axiom of choice or even
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and you still want to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} found
            mathematics on some sort of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %h3#InternalCatMotivation Motivation from internal categories
          %p
            Since questions concerning the axiom of choice tend to look a bit esoteric to those not actively interested in questions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            theory, where the concept is of independent use, and in fact well known by other names than “anafunctor”.
          %p
            Consider some ambient category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to which we want to do category theory. A good example to keep in mind is the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            between topological spaces has a
            %em continuous
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
          %p
            Then it may easily happen that an internal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon C \to D
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal categories
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            (for instance between topological categories) is fully faithful, in the “internal” sense that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{C_1 &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; D_1\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                  C_0 \times C_0 &amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; D_0\times D_0}
          %p
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 0
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \times_{D_0} Iso(D) \to D_0
            is surjective (or even a quotient map, i.e. a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(\mathcal{E})
          %p
            For example, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a topological space and consider the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U) \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is regarded as an internal category in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
            in the categorical sense, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+nerve"} Cech groupoid
            associated to an open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \bigcup_i U_i
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i U_i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_{i,j} (U_i\cap U_j)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)\to X
            is fully faithful (essentially by definition of the morphisms in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            are a cover of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i U_i \to X
            does not have a continuous section if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is connected unless one of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            is already equal to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Note that the foundational point of view also fits in this picture; we can simply take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi AC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} = Set_{\not AC}
            to be the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h3#HomotopicalMotivation Homotopical motivation
          %p
            There is a standard way to deal with such situations where we are faced with a category – here the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(\mathcal{E})
            of categories internal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            – some of whose morphisms look like they ought to have inverses, but do not: we call these would-be invertible morphisms
            %em weak equivalences
            such that our category becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
          %p
            Here we take the weak equivalences in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(\mathcal{E})
            to be the internal functors that are internally fully faithful and essentially surjective. It turns out that this choice of weak equivalences is particularly well-behaved in that it actually forms a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+fractions"} calculus of fractions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            by Gabriel and Zisman, there is simple explicit construction of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Cat(\mathcal{E}))
            in this case: the objects are the same as those of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(\mathcal{E})
            – hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} categories internal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            for us – and the morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon C \to D
            are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(\mathcal{E})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mover
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\hat C &amp;\stackrel{\hat f}{\to}&amp; D
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}}
                  \\\
                  C
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the left leg is a weak equivalence, hence for us: where the left leg is an internal functor that is
            = succeed "-surjective" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer-Kan+localization"} Dwyer-Kan localization
            at our chosen weak equivalences.)
          %p
            For the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo =
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} = Top
            such a span is a morphism out of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em Cech cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = X
            a topological space regarded as a topological category, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \mathbf{B}G
            its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            one-object topological groupoid, such a span is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cocycle
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with values in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            And finally: for the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} = Set_{\not C}
            is the category of sets without the axiom of choice, such a span is an
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C \to C
            that is surjective on objects and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C \to D
            out of the “resolution” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p So one can understand ordinary anafunctors as follows:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                first we consider that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                may fail, which makes previously invertible functors non-invertible;
            %li
              %p
                then we universally
                %em force
                the now non-invertible functors to become invertible after all, by throwing in formal inverses for them.
          %p
            More generally, in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            the morphisms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            are represented by
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ≃
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\hat X &amp;\to &amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}}
                  \\\
                  X
                  }
          %p
            with the left leg being an acyclic fibration. (This is a special case of the general statements of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Given categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %strong anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C \to D
            may be rather slickly defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            of ordinary (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functor"}> strict
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ¯
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi τ
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \overset{\sigma}\leftarrow \overline{F} \overset{\tau}\rightarrow D
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{F}
            is some category), with the property that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\colon {\overline{F}} \to C
            is both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
            and (strictly!) surjective on both objects and morphisms (therefore both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective on objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is merely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} weak equivalence
            (that is, faithful, full, and essentially surjective on objects), although that is slightly more complicated to work with.
          %h3#SetDef Explicit set-theoretic definition
          %p
            In more explicit detail, an
            %strong anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C \to D
            consists of:
          %ul
            %li
              a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|F|}
              of
              %strong specifications
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              (which corresponds to the set of objects of
              = succeed ");" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi F
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{F}
            %li
              maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\colon {|F|} \to C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau\colon {|F|} \to D
              (taking values in objects). Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon C
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\colon D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is a
              %strong specified value
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              if, for some
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\colon {|F|}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = \sigma(s)
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = \tau(s)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              %strong specifies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              as a value of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s(x) = y
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s(x) \coloneqq \tau(s) .
              %p
                We say that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                is a
                %strong value
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                is isomorphic (in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                at
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x) \cong y
                %em the
                value of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
