          %h3#internal_definition_using_covers Internal definition using covers
          %p We generalise the slick definition of anafunctors as spans rather than the detailed definition involving specified values.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a category containing a collection of morphisms called “covers” such that
          %ul
            %li
              every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              is a cover,
            %li covers are closed under composition,
            %li
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              of a cover exists and is a cover (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+stability"}> pullback stability
              ),
            %li
              every cover is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotient object
              of its
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism"} effective epimorphism
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
          %p
            Note that these are precisely the axioms saying that the singleton families
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p\colon V\to U\}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is a cover form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+coverage"} subcanonical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"} Grothendieck pretopology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            and the covers are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the category of smooth manifolds and the covers are the surjective submersions.
          %p
            In such a situation, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal categories
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
            to consist of a span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\leftarrow F \to D
            of internal functors such that:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0\to C_0
                (the map of objects) is a cover.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to C
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ff+morphism"} fully-faithful
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi F
                                %mn 1
                            %mtd
                              %mo →
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi C
                                %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi F
                                %mn 0
                              %mo ×
                              %msub
                                %mi F
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo →
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi C
                                %mn 0
                              %mo ×
                              %msub
                                %mi C
                                %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{F_1 &amp; \to &amp; C_1 \\ \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow \\ F_0\times F_0&amp;   \to &amp; C_0\times C_0}
          %p
            Note that assuming
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0\to C_0
            is a cover, so is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0\times F_0\to C_0\times C_0
            (it is a composition of pullbacks of
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0\to C_0
          %p
            By the remarks above, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            and “cover” means “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"}> surjection
            ” (an example where the covers are the regular epimorphisms), then we recover the original external notion of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small
            ) anafunctor. An anafunctor, defined in this way, is saturated just when the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} core(F) \to core(C\times D)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cores"} cores
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to D
            is fully faithful and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0\times_{C_0}C_1 \to C_0
            has splits over a cover of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalences"} Morita equivalences
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\leftarrow F \to D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ←
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\leftarrow G \to D
            are internal anafunctors, we define an
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ananatural+transformation"} ananatural transformation
            between them (or simply a
            = succeed "," do
              %em natural transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            between the two induced internal functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\times_C G \to D
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %span.newWikiWord
              metalogic
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/metalogic"} ?
            to conclude this, depending on whether there is a natural way to choose the necessary pullbacks; else you get an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anabicategory"} anabicategory
          %p The role of the assumptions about covers is:
          %ul
            %li Identity maps must be covers in order to have identity anafunctors (and more generally, for every functor to give rise to an anafunctor).
            %li To compose anafunctors by pullback, the pullbacks of covers must exist and be covers, and covers must be closed under composition.
            %li
              To define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \times_C G
              and obtain a notion of natural transformation, we again need covers to have pullbacks.
            %li
              To define composition of natural transformations between anafunctors, we need covers to be effective; see diagram (118) in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#HGT1"} HGT1
          %p
            Note: in Section 1.1.5 of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HGT1"} HGT1
          %ul
            %li
              every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
              involving a cover has a quotient object which is a cover.
          %p
            This is not needed for anafunctors but is used to relate descent to bundles (and then to
            = succeed "-bundles)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %h3#as_an_operation_on_2categories As an operation on 2-categories
          %p
            %em
              This section is work in progress by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"} me
          %p
            While internal anafunctors seem to require a lot of baggage, they can be defined very elegantly by working with the 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(S)
            (or some sub-2-category thereof) as a 2-category. One first makes the observation:
          %ul
            %li
              functors internal to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              which are fully faithful and whose object component belongs to a singleton Grothendieck pretopology themselves form a (strict) singleton pretopology on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(S)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ff+morphism"} ff
          %h3#homotopytheoretic_interpretation Homotopy-theoretic interpretation
          %p
            Observe that the surjective-on-objects equivalences are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} acyclic fibrations
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            whose first leg is not just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            but an acyclic fibration. However, it appears that the canonical model structure on Cat only exists (with its weak equivalences being the fully faithful and essentially surjective maps) under the assumption of some choice—though full AC is not needed,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            suffices.
          %p
            More generally, it is proven in
            %a{:href => "#EKV"} EKV
            that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            has a Grothendieck coverage, then under suitable additional conditions on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (and, of course, the axiom of choice assumed external to
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model structure
            on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(S)
            of internal categories in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            relative to that coverage. The internal anafunctors relative to the given coverage, as defined above, can then once again be identified with the spans whose first leg is an acyclic fibration.
          %p
            Since all objects in the canonical model structure on Cat are fibrant, according to Kenneth Brown’s theorem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+cohomology+theory"} homotopical cohomology theory
            it follows that one-step generalized morphisms already realize the full localization, i.e. they represent all morphisms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Cat)
          %p
            If we specialize to groupoids, with their canonical model structure by
            = succeed "\u2013" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Brown
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marek+Golasi%C5%84ski"} Golasiński
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+cohomology+theory"} homotopical cohomology theory
            this means that anafunctors between groupoids represent
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cocycle"} nonabelian cocycle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} codescent
            such homotopical cocycles are related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent data
            that enters the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            and
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            We will use the
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor#SetDef"} explicit set-theoretic definition
            in this section.
          %h3#product_anafunctor Product anafunctor
          %p
            Given a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} binary products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P: C \times C \to C
            that sends a pair of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, B)
            to a product of them. This requires picking a product for each pair, and hence requires the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            However, we can form the product anafunctor without using choice. The specifications are given by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mtext product diagrams in
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |P| = \text{product diagrams in }\;C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: |P| \to C \times C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau: |P| \to C
            are given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ←
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ←
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma(A \leftarrow D \rightarrow B) = (A, B),\;\; \tau(A \leftarrow D \rightarrow B) = D.
          %p
            Given product diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ←
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \leftarrow D \rightarrow B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ←
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A' \leftarrow D' \rightarrow B'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, g): (A, B) \to (A', C')
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \times C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(f, g): D \to D'
            by the universal property of the product. The compatibility conditions are easy to check.
          %h3#anafunctor_from_functor Anafunctor from functor
          %p
            Suppose we have a usual functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C &amp; \overset{F}\to &amp; D\\
                  \\mathllap{id_C} \downarrow \\
                  C
                  }
          %p The composition of anafunctors agree with the composition of functors.
          %h3#inverses_of_anafunctors Inverses of anafunctors
          %p
            Given an anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: |F| \to C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau: |F| \to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            (ie.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
          %p
            Then given a saturated full and faithful essentially surjective anafunctor, we can obtain an inverse anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^{-1}: D \to C
            by swapping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            around. The conditions of being full and faithful and essentially surjective guarantees the axioms are still satisfied.
          %h2#SizeQuestions Questions of size
          %p
            Even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ana
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D)
            of anafunctors from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is not necessarily even essentially small, and thus the 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi ana
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}
            of categories and anafunctors is not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HenryMO"} this MO discussion
          %p
            This is true, however, under the assumption of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Cat)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\leftarrow F \to D
            is equivalent to one where the set of objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0'\to C_0
            is a projective cover of the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            of objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            COSHEP is actually stronger than necessary for this; all that is really needed is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WISC"} WISC
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/X
            consisting of surjections has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+initial+set"} weakly initial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\leftarrow F \to D
            is equivalent to one where the set of objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+multiple+choice"} axiom of multiple choice
            and the axiom of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+violations+of+choice"} small violations of choice
            (SVC).
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Makkai"} Makkai’s paper
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ana
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D)
            is essentially small under the assumption of his
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+cardinality+selection+axiom"} small cardinality selection axiom
            (SCSA), which also follows from SVC. Although SCSA and WISC carry the same feel that “choice is violated only in a small way,” Makkai’s proof from SCSA is an “injective” approach, in that the set of possibilities for the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is constructed mainly from
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as in the “projective” approach above using COSHEP or WISC.
          %p
            Another axiom ensuring that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ana
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D)
            is essentially small is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+stack+completions"} axiom of stack completions
            (ASC), since if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to \hat{D}
            is an intrinsic stack completion we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ana
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Fun
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ana(C,D) \simeq Fun(C,\hat{D})
          %p
            In particular, the bicategory of categories and anafunctors is locally essentially small and cartesian closed in the internal logic of any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %h2#anafunctors_in_homotopy_type_theory Anafunctors in homotopy type theory
          %p
            Anafunctors are unnecessary when using “saturated/univalent” categories in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (see Def. 9.1.3 of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+HoTT+book"} the HoTT book
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+comprehension+principle"} functor comprehension principle
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %h3#anafunctors_versus_representable_profunctors Anafunctors versus representable profunctors
          %p
            A different way of describing “a functor whose values are determined only up to isomorphism” is with a representable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
            (a.k.a. distributor). A profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A &amp;#8696; B
            is a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon A \to [B^{op},Set]
            %em representable
            if each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(a)
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
          %p
            In the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} AC
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_a\in B
            for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and thereby define a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            are “representations” of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(a)
          %p
            Conversely, given an anafunctor represented by a span
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi g
                    %mi P
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \xleftarrow{g} P \xrightarrow{f} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A &amp;#8696; B
            as the composite
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* \circ g^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*(p)(b) = B(b,f(p))
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_*(a)(p) = A(g(p),a)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalence of 2-categories
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi ana
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Prof
                      %mi rep
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof_{rep}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+full+sub-2-category"} locally full sub-2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            determined by the representable profunctors. (This appears to have been written down first
            %a{:href => "http://permalink.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/6485"} here
            by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Benabou"} Jean Benabou
          %p
            Anafunctors and representable profunctors each have advantages. For purposes which require only the 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi ana
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Prof
                      %mi rep
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}\simeq Prof_{rep}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cleavage"} cloven
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
            can equally well be turned into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            valued in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi ana
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}
            or in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Prof
                      %mi rep
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof_{rep}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Prof
                    %mi rep
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof_{rep}
            is arguably more natural, since an arbitrary functor (not necessarily a fibration)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
            can be turned into a normal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+2-functor"} lax 2-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Prof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}\to Prof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Prof
                    %mi rep
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof_{rep}
            exactly when the given functor was a fibration. (It is a pseudofunctor landing in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Prof
                    %mi corep
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof_{corep}
            iff the functor was an opfibration, and it is a pseudofunctor iff the functor was
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+functor"} exponentiable
          %p
            On the other hand, sometimes it requires a little contortion to put something in the form of a representable distributor. For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has binary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\colon A \times A  &amp;#8696; A
            defined by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a_1,a_2)(a) = Hom_A(a,a_1) \times Hom_A(a,a_2)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mi ⇸
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\colon  A \times A  &amp;#8696; A
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(a_1,a_2)(a)=
              the set of triples
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_3,p_1,p_2,f)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i\colon a_i \to a_3
              are the injections into a coproduct and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a morphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to a_3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_3,p_1,p_2,f) \sim (a_3',p_1',p_2',f')
              if there exists a (necessarily unique iso)morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon a_3 \to a_3'
              commuting with all the structure maps.
          %p
            (This is essentially making explicit the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi ana
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Prof
                    %mi rep
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana} \to Prof_{rep}
            defined above.) Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is more easily defined as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corepresentable+functor"} corepresentable
            distributor. But if we want to define a functor that involves both limits and colimits, like
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b,c) \mapsto a \times (b + c)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo ←
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times A \leftarrow P \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is the category of binary product diagrams in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo ←
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times A \leftarrow C \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of binary coproduct diagrams. And in the third case, we consider the span
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo ←
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times A\times A \leftarrow D \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is the category of binary coproduct diagrams together with a product diagram one of whose factors is the vertex of the coproduct diagram.
          %p
            Roughly speaking, anafunctors are formulated exactly in order to describe “functors defined up to isomorphism,” while representable distributors describe “functors valued in representable presheaves.” “Objects defined up to isomorphism” and “representable presheaves” are
            %em formally
            equivalent (without invoking AC), but not every “naturally occurring” object-defined-up-to-isomorphism is “given in nature” by the presheaf it represents. Some are given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            they corepresent; others aren’t given directly in either of those ways. One can also think of an anafunctor as a particularly convenient “presentation” of a representable distributor.
          %p
            A further reason that the notion of anafunctor is useful is that when working with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            categories or groupoids, profunctors are not so much an option, but anafunctors are well-behaved (see the papers by Bartels and Roberts referenced below).
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %h3#higher_versions Higher versions
          %p
            see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-anafunctor"} infinity-anafunctor
          %h3#lower_version Lower version
          %p
            see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunction"} anafunction
          %h3#additive_version Additive version
          %p
            The notion of abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/butterfly"} butterfly
            introduced by Behrang Noohi
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0506313"} Weak maps of 2-groups
            is the additive version of the notion of (saturated) anafunctor: the equivalence between, on the one hand, internal groupoids and internal functors and, on the other hand, arrows and commutative squares in an abelian category extends to an equivalence between saturated anafunctors and butterflies.
          %h2#references References
          %p The term “anafunctor” was introduced by Michael Makkai in
          %ul
            %li#Makkai
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"}> Michael Makkai
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Avoiding the axiom of choice in general category theory
              %strong 108
              isse 2 (1996) pp 109-173, doi:
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049%2895%2900029-1"}> 10.1016/0022-4049(95)00029-1
              , (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/makkai/anafun/"}> author’s page
              )
          %p motivated in part to complete the analogy prophase:anaphase::profunctor:??.
          %p The concept also appears, unnamed, in the article
          %p#Kelly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
            ,
            %em Complete functors in homology I
            (1963)
          %p
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
          %p
            The popularity of the term was notably pushed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
          %ul#HGT1
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"}> Toby Bartels
              ,
              %em Higher Gauge Theory I: 2-Bundles
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0410328"}> arXiv:math.CT/0410328
              )
          %p
            A development and exposition of the general setup taking Makkai’s and Bartels’ motivations and the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical categories
            into account is
          %ul
            %li#Roberts12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal categories, anafunctors and localisations
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theory+and+Applications+of+Categories"} Theory and Applications of Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/26/29/26-29abs.html"} journal version
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.2363"} arXiv:1101.2363
          %p and a purely formal construction using 2-categories appears in the notes
          %ul
            %li#Roberts18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              ,
              %em The construction of formal anafunctors
              (2018) arXiv:
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.04552"}> 1808.04552
              , doi:
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.25909/5b6cfd1a73e55"} 10.25909/5b6cfd1a73e55
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally cartesian closed categories without chosen constructions
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/20/1/20-01abs.html"} TAC
          %p Since anafunctors are a special case of a more general concept, they, or the general theory applying to them, has been considered under different terms elsewhere.
          %p
            The general question of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structures on categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal categories
            is discussed in
          %ul#EKV
            %li
              T. Everaert, R.W. Kieboom and T. Van der Linden ,
              %em Model structures for homotopy of internal categories
              TAC, Vol. 15, CT2004, No. 3, pp 66-94. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/15/3/15-03abs.html"} web
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/15/3/15-03.pdf"}> pdf
              ))
          %p Closely related, still a bit more general, are the considerations in
          %ul
            %li
              Jardine,
              %em Cocycle categories
              K-theory 0782 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0782/"}> web
              ) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0782/coc-cat3.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Some models of set theory in which the bicategory of anafunctors fails to be small are sketched in the answers to
          %ul
            %li#HenryMO
              Simon Henry,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Non smallness of the set of anafunctors without AC?
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/264585"} https://mathoverflow.net/q/264585
        .revisedby
